<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Xiaoting</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">221–<a href="/years/222">222</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Battle of Yiling</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Xiaoting, also called the Battle of Yiling, was fought in 221–222 in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> of Shu Han and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a>'s Eastern Wu. Liu Bei invaded to avenge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-yu">Guan Yu</a> and recover Jing province, but the Wu commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun">Lu Xun</a> waited until summer and then destroyed his strung-out camps with fire. Liu Bei fled and died the next year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-xiaoting.jpg" alt="Battle of Xiaoting" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Yiling.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In late 219 Lü Meng, a general serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a>, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a>'s territories in southern Jing Province while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-yu">Guan Yu</a>, who guarded them, was away at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fancheng">Fancheng</a>; Guan Yu was captured in an ambush and executed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-pi">Cao Pi</a> founded the state of Wei in December 220, Liu Bei declared himself emperor of the Han on 10 May 221, and on 23 September 221 Sun Quan became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Wei with the title King of Wu. Zhao Yun and the official Qin Mi advised against war, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-fei">Zhang Fei</a> was assassinated by his own subordinates while mobilising, and peace talks through Zhuge Jin came to nothing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In August 221 Wu Ban and Feng Xi took the Wu positions at the Wu Gorge, and the Shu army of over 40,000 troops advanced to Zigui County. Sun Quan gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun">Lu Xun</a> 50,000 troops as Grand Chief Controller. In February 222 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> moved along the southern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, leaving Huang Quan to guard the northern bank against Wei, and built about 10 fortified garrisons at the border of Yiling. Lu Xun refused his officers' calls to attack and saw through a lure in which 8,000 Shu troops lay in ambush in the valleys.</p><p>When he struck, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun">Lu Xun</a> had each man carry straw for a fire attack and followed it with an all-out assault. Feng Xi, Zhang Nan, Ma Liang and the tribal king Shamoke were killed, and over 40 Shu camps were destroyed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> fell back to the Ma'an Hills, where landslides cost him thousands more, and escaped at night through the mountains to Yufu County, which he renamed Yong'an. His boats, equipment and supplies were all captured.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Huang Quan's army on the northern bank was cut off and defected to Wei. On the advice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun">Lu Xun</a>, Zhu Ran and Luo Tong, who distrusted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-pi">Cao Pi</a>'s intentions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> did not pursue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baidicheng">Baidicheng</a>, and Cao Pi soon invaded Wu from three directions. Before his death on 10 June 223 Liu Bei named his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shan">Liu Shan</a> as successor, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a> and Li Yan as regents. Zhuge Liang made peace with Wu and restored the Wu–Shu alliance against Wei.</p><p>The campaign is told in chapters 81–84 of the 14th-century novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms</a> by Luo Guanzhong, which adds fictitious episodes; according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Sanguozhi</a>, Huang Zhong died in 220 and Zhao Yun took no part in the battle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Xiaoting” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sun Ce's brother Sun Quan defeated Cao Cao at Red Cliffs with Liu Bei in 208, took Jing province from Guan Yu in 219 and beat Liu Bei at <b>Yiling</b> in 222.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-wu">Eastern Wu</a></li><li><blockquote>Liu Bei's attack on Eastern Wu ended in disaster at <b>Yiling</b> in 222, and he died in 223.</blockquote><a href="/state/shu-han">Shu Han</a></li></ul></section>
